//@ expect: reject
//@ reason: positive control -- `n[key] = value;` on a class-typed receiver is
//@ reason: rejected even though `operator[]` itself exists and works fine in
//@ reason: READ position (see operator_subscript_read_accepted.clg). Real C++
//@ reason: needs operator[] to return a REFERENCE for the subsequent `=` to
//@ reason: assign through, and CeeLing has no `ref` return type yet (TODO.md/
//@ reason: Ch. 9) -- rejected with a targeted diagnostic pointing at the real
//@ reason: limitation, not silently emitted as something that fails to
//@ reason: compile or (worse) compiles into the wrong thing.

class Box {
public:
    int val;
    Box(int v) { val = v; }
    int operator[](string key) {
        return val;
    }
};

int main() {
    Box b = new Box(5);
    b["key"] = 10;
    return 0;
}
